.TH "vsearch-pairwise_alignment_parameters" "7" "April 29, 2026" "version 2.31.0" "vsearch file formats"
.hy
.SH NAME
.PP
pairwise alignment parameters \[em] a description of the pairwise
alignment model implemented in vsearch
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
vsearch implements an extremely fast Needleman-Wunsch algorithm, making
use of the Streaming SIMD Extensions (SSE2) of post-2003 x86-64 CPUs.
On Power8 CPUs it uses AltiVec/VSX/VMX instructions, and on ARMv8 CPUs
it uses Neon instructions.
On other systems it can use the SIMD Everywhere (simde) library, if
available.
For comparisons involving sequences with a length product greater than
25 million (e.g.\ two sequences of length 5 kb), vsearch uses a slower
alignment method described by Hirschberg (1975) and Myers and Miller
(1988), with much smaller memory requirements.
.PP
The Needleman-Wunsch algorithm performs \f[I]global\f[R] pairwise
alignment: it aligns two sequences end-to-end, over their full lengths.
This differs from Smith-Waterman, which performs \f[I]local\f[R]
alignment and identifies the highest-scoring matching subsequence.
vsearch always uses global alignment.
.PP
Along the pairwise alignment, each aligned position contributes to the
score, by being either a \f[I]match\f[R], a \f[I]mismatch\f[R], or a
\f[I]gap\f[R].
.PP
vsearch interprets symbols in DNA/RNA sequences according to the IUPAC
coding system for nucleotides.
See \f[V]vsearch-nucleotides(7)\f[R] for details.
.PP
The alignment score is used solely to find the optimal alignment.
The similarity percentage reported (and compared against \f[V]--id\f[R])
is computed from the resulting alignment, not from the score itself.
The identity definition can be changed with \f[V]--iddef\f[R] (see
below).
.SS Matches and mismatches
.PP
When aligning sequences, identical symbols will receive a positive match
score (default +2, see option \f[V]--match\f[R]).
Note that T and U are considered identical, regardless of their case.
If two symbols are not identical, their alignment results in a negative
mismatch score (default -4, see option \f[V]--mismatch\f[R]).
Aligning a pair of symbols where at least one of them is an ambiguous
symbol (BDHKMNRSVWY) will always result in a score of zero.
Alignment of two identical ambiguous symbols (for example, R vs R) also
receives a score of zero.
As an exception, when \f[V]--n_mismatch\f[R] is given, any pairing
involving an \f[V]N\f[R] is scored as a mismatch instead of zero.
.PP
Once the optimal pairwise alignment has been found, when computing the
amount of similarity by counting matches and mismatches \f[B]after\f[R]
alignment, ambiguous nucleotide symbols will count as matching to other
symbols if they have at least one of the nucleotides (ACGTU) they may
represent in common.
For example: W will match A and T, but also any of MRVHDN.
When showing alignments (for example with the output option
\f[V]--alnout\f[R]) matches involving ambiguous symbols will be shown
with a plus character (+) between them while exact matches between
non-ambiguous symbols will be shown with a vertical bar character (|).
.SS Gaps
.PP
Gaps are further refined into \f[I]gap openings\f[R] (see option
\f[V]--gapopen\f[R]) or \f[I]gap extensions\f[R] (see option
\f[V]--gapext\f[R]).
Gaps are asymmetrical: a gap opening can occur in six different
contexts: in the query (Q) or in the target (T) sequence; inside the
sequence (I), or at the left (L) or right (R) extremity.
.SS Gap openings
.PP
Sequence symbols (Q and T) can be combined with location symbols (L, I,
and R), and numerical values to declare penalties for all possible
contexts: \f[V]aQL/bQI/cQR/dTL/eTI/fTR\f[R], where \f[I]abcdef\f[R] are
zero or positive integers, and \f[V]/\f[R] is used as a separator.
.TP
\f[V]--gapopen\f[R] \f[I]2QL/20QI/2QR/2TL/20TI/2TR\f[R]
Set the six gap opening penalties using a penalty of 20 for opening
internal gaps and a penalty of 2 for opening terminal gaps, in both
query and target sequences.
This is the default.
.PP
To simplify declarations, the symbol (E) can be used to treat both
extremities (L and R) equally \[em] combining E with L or R in the same
penalty string is a fatal error \[em] and the symbols Q and T can be
omitted to treat query and target sequences equally.
.TP
\f[V]--gapopen\f[R] \f[I]20I/2E\f[R]
Set the six gap opening penalties using a penalty of 20 for opening
internal gaps and a penalty of 2 for opening terminal gaps, in both
query and target sequences.
This is the default.
.PP
If only a numerical value is given, without any sequence or location
symbol, then the penalty applies to all gap openings.
For example:
.TP
\f[V]--gapopen\f[R] \f[I]20\f[R]
Set the six gap opening penalties using a penalty of 20 for all gap
openings, internal or terminal, in both query and target sequences.
.PP
To forbid a gap opening, an infinite penalty value can be declared with
the symbol \f[V]*\f[R]: a gap opening whose penalty is infinite is never
allowed.
Like a numerical penalty, \f[V]*\f[R] applies to the selected sequence
(Q or T) and location (L, I, R or E); a bare \f[V]*\f[R] forbids every
gap opening (internal and terminal, in both sequences).
.TP
\f[V]--gapopen\f[R] \f[I]*I/2E\f[R]
Set the gap opening penalties to an infinite value for internal gap
openings, in both query and target sequences.
.TP
\f[V]--gapopen\f[R] \f[I]*LQ\f[R]
Forbid the opening of left end-gaps in the query sequence; all other gap
opening penalties keep their default values.
.PP
To use vsearch as a semi-global aligner, a null-penalty can be applied
to the left (L) or right (R) gaps.
.PP
vsearch always initializes the six gap opening penalties using the
default parameters (20I/2E).
The user is then free to declare only the values they want to modify.
.PP
The string is scanned from left to right, accepted symbols are
\f[V]0123456789/LIREQT*\f[R], and later values override previous values.
.PP
Each finite penalty must be an integer between 0 and 6553.
To declare a larger, gap-forbidding penalty, use the infinite value
\f[V]*\f[R] (see above).
.PP
Please note that vsearch, in contrast to usearch, only allows integer
gap penalties.
Because the lowest gap penalties are 0.5 by default in usearch, all
default scores and gap penalties in vsearch have been doubled to
maintain equivalent penalties and to produce identical alignments.
.SS Gap extensions
.PP
Gap extensions follow the same penalty declaration system as gap
openings.
.TP
\f[V]--gapext\f[R] \f[I]2I/1E\f[R]
Set the six gap extending penalties using a penalty of 2 for extending
internal gaps and a penalty of 1 for extending terminal gaps, in both
query and target sequences.
This is the default.
.PP
An infinite extension penalty declared with \f[V]*\f[R] forbids
extending a gap of that class beyond a single position: gaps longer than
one are not allowed, while a gap of length one is still permitted.
As with gap openings, \f[V]*\f[R] applies to the selected sequence (Q or
T) and location (L, I, R or E).
.TP
\f[V]--gapext\f[R] \f[I]*I\f[R]
Forbid internal gaps longer than one position, in both query and target
sequences.
.SS Identity definitions
.PP
The identity percentage computed from a pairwise alignment can be
defined in several ways.
The option \f[V]--iddef\f[R] selects the definition used when applying
the \f[V]--id\f[R] threshold:
.TP
\f[V]--iddef\f[R] \f[I]0\f[R]
CD-HIT definition: (matching columns) / (shortest sequence length).
.TP
\f[V]--iddef\f[R] \f[I]1\f[R]
Edit distance: (matching columns) / (alignment length).
.TP
\f[V]--iddef\f[R] \f[I]2\f[R]
Edit distance excluding terminal gaps: (matching columns) / (alignment
length - terminal gaps).
This is the default.
.TP
\f[V]--iddef\f[R] \f[I]3\f[R]
Marine Biological Lab definition, counting each gap opening (internal or
terminal) as a single mismatch, whether or not the gap was extended: 1.0
- [(mismatches + gap openings) / (longest sequence length)].
.TP
\f[V]--iddef\f[R] \f[I]4\f[R]
BLAST definition, equivalent to \f[V]--iddef 1\f[R] for global pairwise
alignments.
.PP
Note that the \f[V]--iddef\f[R] choice has no effect on the score or
selection of the optimal pairwise alignment.
The identity is computed from the alignment after the fact.
